---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevEncodeRadixText from '@/components/tools/dev/DevEncodeRadixText.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="hex-to-text">
  <DevEncodeRadixText client:load radix="hex" direction="decode" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Reading hexadecimal</h2>
    <p>
      Hex is base 16: the digits 0–9, then A–F for ten to fifteen. One hex digit carries exactly four
      bits, so two digits describe one byte. That is why hex is the default way to look at binary data.
      A byte in decimal runs 0–255 with no fixed width. In hex it is always two characters, 00 to FF.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Character</th><th>Hex</th><th>Decimal</th><th>Binary</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>H</code></td><td>48</td><td>72</td><td>01001000</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>e</code></td><td>65</td><td>101</td><td>01100101</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>l</code></td><td>6C</td><td>108</td><td>01101100</td></tr>
          <tr><td>space</td><td>20</td><td>32</td><td>00100000</td></tr>
          <tr><td>line feed</td><td>0A</td><td>10</td><td>00001010</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>UTF-8 versus single-byte decoding</h2>
    <p>
      Most hex to text decoders go wrong here. Modern text is almost always UTF-8, where each character
      outside plain ASCII spans several bytes:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>é</code> is <code>C3 A9</code> (two bytes).</li>
      <li><code>€</code> is <code>E2 82 AC</code> (three bytes).</li>
      <li><code>😀</code> is <code>F0 9F 98 80</code> (four bytes).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A single-byte reader turns those into mojibake: <code>Ã©</code> in place of <code>é</code>. If you
      see that, switch the character set to UTF-8. If you get replacement characters in UTF-8 mode, the
      data is probably Latin-1, or not text.
    </p>

    <h2>Input formats this accepts</h2>
    <p>All of the following decode to <code>Hi</code>:</p>
    <pre><code>4869
48 69
48,69
48:69
0x48 0x69
\x48\x69</code></pre>
    <p>
      Any character that is not a hex digit is a separator, so you can paste from a spec, a config file
      or a chat message. A full hex dump with offsets and an ASCII column does not work, because the
      parser reads those columns as data. Remove them first.
    </p>

    <h2>Common things you will find in a hex blob</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Bytes</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>EF BB BF</code> at the start</td><td>UTF-8 byte-order mark. It is invisible and causes many parser errors</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>0D 0A</code></td><td>Windows line ending (CRLF). <code>0A</code> alone is Unix</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>C2 A0</code></td><td>Non-breaking space. It looks like a space but is a different character</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>00</code> padding at the end</td><td>A fixed-length field, not part of the string</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>FF FE</code> at the start</td><td>UTF-16 little-endian BOM. The data is not UTF-8</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Where hex shows up</h2>
    <p>
      Colour values in CSS, MAC addresses, hashes and checksums, memory addresses in a stack trace,
      packet captures, embedded-device protocol traces, Unicode codepoints, and the raw view in each hex
      editor.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
